# Paw Paw Picnic — Development Process

## 1. Starting Idea

I wanted to create a relaxing and playful 3D game where players could enjoy exploring without time limits or competitive pressure.

My initial idea was to let players control a cute puppy, explore a miniature world, collect ingredients, and prepare food for a picnic.

I chose a picnic box as the main visual concept because I wanted the game to feel like opening a childhood toy box, filled with small environments and playful surprises.

The main interaction loop is simple: choose a recipe, explore different locations, collect ingredients, receive feedback, and prepare the final food.

## 2. Design and Code Iteration

In the early versions, the ingredient collection methods were too repetitive. Although the interactions worked, collecting different ingredients felt almost the same.

I decided to improve this by introducing different collection methods, such as picking fruit, searching for mushrooms, and gathering crops.

The early 3D environments were also relatively simple, and some objects did not match my intended visual style. I repeatedly adjusted the models, plants, decorations, and scene layouts to make each location feel more distinctive.

I also improved the interaction system by adding recipe selection, inventory tracking, ingredient location hints, and collection feedback.

These changes helped me create a more complete experience where players could understand their progress and decide what to do next.

## 3. User Testing

I invited another person to try Paw Paw Picnic and observed how they interacted with the game.

During the test, two main usability problems were identified.

**Problem 1: Missing Interaction Feedback**

Some buttons did not provide clear feedback after being clicked. This made it difficult for the player to understand whether their actions were successful or what to do next.

After the test, I fixed these interactions to provide clearer feedback, helping players understand the results of their actions.

**Problem 2: Mobile Screen Compatibility**

When the player opened the game on a mobile phone, the webpage did not properly adapt to the smaller screen. This affected the layout and made the game more difficult to use.

I improved the responsive layout so that the interface could better fit mobile screens.

**What I Learned from Testing**

This test helped me realise that an interaction that seems clear to the developer may not be obvious to a first-time player. It also showed me the importance of testing a game on different devices.

By fixing these problems, I improved the usability of the game and made the experience more accessible to different players.

## 4. AI-Assisted Development

I developed Paw Paw Picnic using JavaScript, Three.js, and Vite, with assistance from **ChatGPT and OpenAI Codex**.

I used ChatGPT to explore game ideas, plan the gameplay, discuss visual design, and identify problems during development. I used Codex to assist with writing, modifying, and debugging code, including building 3D environments and implementing interactive features.

However, AI-generated results did not always match my expectations. In the early versions, ingredient collection methods were too repetitive, the environments were too simple, and some objects did not match my intended visual style.

**An AI-assisted approach I kept:** I kept the approach of using Three.js and procedural modelling to create a miniature 3D world that runs directly in a browser.

**An AI-generated result I changed:** I revised the early repetitive ingredient collection interactions and introduced different ways to collect ingredients, making the gameplay more varied and engaging.

Throughout development, I tested the results, identified problems, and guided AI to make further improvements.

This experience helped me realise that **AI can help me write code, but it cannot replace my design decisions.** My responsibility is to define the experience I want to create, evaluate the results, and guide the project towards my intended direction.

## 5. Reflection

This project helped me better understand the relationship between programming and interaction design.

I learned that making a feature work is not the same as creating a good user experience. It is also important to consider whether players understand the controls, receive clear feedback, and know what to do next.

For me, programming is not only a tool for implementing design ideas, but also a process of testing, identifying problems, and improving the experience.
